# =============================================================================
# terraform/.gitignore
# =============================================================================
# This applies to every module under terraform/ (bootstrap, modules/*,
# environments/*). A few of these matter enough to explain individually:
# =============================================================================

# The .terraform/ directory is Terraform's local cache — downloaded provider
# plugins (the AWS provider binary itself is 100+MB) and module references.
# It's regenerated by `terraform init` on any machine, so committing it would
# just bloat the repo for zero benefit.
.terraform/
.terraform.lock.hcl.bak

# State files: NEVER commit these, even for the local-state bootstrap module.
# State can contain plaintext secrets (DB passwords, API keys pulled into
# resource attributes) and is, by design, meant to live in the S3 backend —
# not in git history, where "deleting" a file doesn't actually erase it from
# past commits.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfstate.backup

# Real variable files contain account-specific / potentially sensitive
# values. We commit `*.tfvars.example` as documentation of what's expected,
# but never the real `*.tfvars` a person actually fills in and runs with.
*.tfvars
!*.tfvars.example

# Terraform sometimes writes crash logs here if something goes seriously
# wrong mid-apply — useful for debugging locally, irrelevant to version
# control.
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Some workflows use -var-file with an override file for local experiments —
# these are, by convention, never meant to be shared/committed.
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json

# CLI configuration can contain credentials/tokens in some setups.
.terraformrc
terraform.rc